(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-medical-literature-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Medical Literature Master Exam — Form D","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does incidence describe?","choices":["Everyone who has the condition at one moment","The distribution of a condition by person and place","The proportion of cases that prove fatal","New cases arising in previously healthy people"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, over a specified time period. That restriction to the previously healthy is what separates it from prevalence, which counts everyone who has the condition. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A drug works well in a tightly monitored trial where nearly everyone takes every dose, but poorly in clinics where many patients miss doses. How do its efficacy and effectiveness compare?","choices":["Efficacy is low and effectiveness is high","Both efficacy and effectiveness are low","Efficacy is high and effectiveness is low","Both efficacy and effectiveness are high"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: efficacy reflects ideal circumstances, such as full compliance, while effectiveness reflects ordinary circumstances, including missed doses. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which pairing best contrasts statistical and clinical significance?","choices":["Random error versus systematic error","Validity versus reliability of a test","Precision versus accuracy of measurement","Chance versus relevance to patients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: statistical significance asks how likely chance is as an explanation, while clinical significance asks whether the difference matters to practice. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What determines how much parallel testing improves sensitivity?","choices":["How well each catches what the other misses","The prevalence of the disease","How expensive the tests are","How specific each test is"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: two tests that fail on the same patients add little, whereas complementary tests between them catch far more of the cases. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A clinical question asks whether nebulized hypertonic saline, compared with normal saline, shortens hospital stay in infants with bronchiolitis. Which element is the outcome?","choices":["Length of hospital stay","Nebulized hypertonic saline","Normal saline","Infants with bronchiolitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because the outcome is what one hopes to accomplish, measure, improve or affect, here how long the infants remain in the hospital. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What limits the diagnosis of disease?","choices":["The prevalence of the disease alone","How good the tests used to measure it are","The number of tests ordered","The cost of the gold standard"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the rest follows from it: sensitivity and specificity guide test selection, while predictive values describe what a given result means. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is a systematic review built to answer?","choices":["As many related questions as the literature allows","How common a condition is at one moment","A specific, focused question","Whether an assigned intervention works"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it does so by systematically identifying and evaluating multiple studies rather than by surveying a field impressionistically. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is intraobserver variation?","choices":["A flaw in the measuring instrument","One patient's value changing between measurements","One observer interpreting the same thing differently twice","Two observers interpreting the same thing differently"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with a radiologist reviewing the same radiograph at two different times and reaching different interpretations as the example. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What lies at the start of the trajectory toward evidence-based information?","choices":["Ideas, from observation, biology and reasoning","Case reports","Randomized controlled trials","Systematic reviews"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and they become hypotheses, then observational studies, then experimental studies, and only then evidence-based information. Imagination is named alongside them. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"In the pyramid, which design sits immediately below randomized controlled trials?","choices":["Systematic reviews","Cohort studies","Case-control studies","Expert opinion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and case-control studies with case series and reports sit below those in turn, so the order rises from case reports through case-control and cohort to randomized trials. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What should happen if evidence of harm, futility or effectiveness emerges during a trial?","choices":["The sample size is quietly reduced","Enrollment continues to the planned end","Only the control group is stopped","The study is ended"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: continuing would expose participants to harm, to a treatment that is not working, or would withhold one already shown to work. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"When does the grading scheme give a weak recommendation?","choices":["Benefits clearly outweigh burdens","Every study reports the same effect","Benefits are closely balanced with risks","Risks clearly outweigh benefits"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and uncertainty in the estimates of benefits, risks and burdens can also lead to a weak grade. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A trial reports a p value of 0.20. Which statement is best supported?","choices":["Eighty percent of patients improved","The treatment has been shown to have no effect","The result is very highly significant","The result is not statistically significant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, since 0.20 is above the threshold of 0.05, so chance remains a reasonable explanation for the difference seen. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does the B of the ABCD method ask about?","choices":["Bias","Breadth of coverage","Backing institution","Bibliography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it sits between the author's credentials and the level of detail in the content, so the method moves from who wrote it to why, then to what and when. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Ice cream sales and drowning deaths both rise in summer, and the link is statistically significant. What does this represent?","choices":["A non-causal association","A necessary cause","A sufficient cause","A dose-response relationship"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the relationship is statistically significant yet no causal relationship exists, because a shared factor such as summer weather drives both. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which are given as examples of field research?","choices":["Testing new screening tests and vaccine trials","Retrospective analysis of hospital discharge records","Systematic reviews of published trials","Interviews conducted for a phenomenological study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and both take place in the population rather than the laboratory, which is what makes them field work during epidemiologic investigation. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why does the severity of a disease matter?","choices":["It removes the need to study outcomes","It sets priorities for health services","It fixes the cost of every treatment","It fixes how many risk factors a patient has"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: knowing how severe the natural course is tells planners where clinical services and public health programs are most needed. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What is the cost of narrowing the population under study?","choices":["It may curtail generalisability","It makes the rate impossible to calculate","It always introduces measurement error","It converts the study into an experiment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the trade is often worth making, as when a study is limited to those in a service region who need the service, but the limit has to be acknowledged. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which standard requires disclosure of members' financial, intellectual, institutional and patient or public interests?","choices":["Transparency","Conflict of interest","Updating","External review"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because group members' conflicts related to financial, intellectual, institutional and patient or public activities bearing on the guideline are disclosed. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What characterizes ordinal data?","choices":["Equal defined intervals between values","Only two possible values","Order among categories without defined intervals","Categories with no order at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: one value can be seen as more, less or equally desirable than another, but the distance between adjacent categories is not defined or equal. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What is the net effect of random variation across many measurements?","choices":["It cannot be estimated or reduced","It biases the result in one direction","It makes the measurement invalid","It balances out, so the true state is not misrepresented"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, although individual measurements can still mislead, which is why averaging reduces it and statistical analysis can estimate it. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What helps establish validity for more subjective measurements?","choices":["Structured questions","Repeating the measurement on another day","Taking the average of several observers","Calibration against a physical standard"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with alcohol screening questionnaires and pain scales as the examples, since neither quantity can be checked against a physical standard. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Where on a survival curve is the estimate most precise?","choices":["Late on, at the right side","Early on, at the left side","Equally at every point along the curve","Wherever the curve plateaus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: more of the population remains under observation early on, so the estimate is based on a larger group. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A study makes 200 comparisons, none reflecting a true difference. If significance is set at a p value of 0.01, about how many will appear significant by chance?","choices":["0","2","20","10"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a p value of 0.01 corresponds to a chance of 1 in 100, and 200 divided by 100 is 2. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which findings are summarized for the studies located in a systematic review?","choices":["Length and language of each report","Journal, year and reputation of the authors","Effect size, precision and validity","Funding source and cost of each study"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and validity is judged both internally (for the sample studied) and externally (for other settings), alongside how many quality studies were found. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"In a study of patients treated with a drug, sicker patients received it more often and also died more often. Which problem does this illustrate?","choices":["Cross-over","Confounding","Effect modification","Placebo effect"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: severity of illness is related both to receiving the drug (the exposure) and to death (the outcome), so it distorts the comparison. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"For secondary prevention, which extra requirement applies to treatment after a condition is found?","choices":["Early treatment works better than later treatment","Treatment carries no cost to patients","Treatment is offered only after symptoms begin","Treatment is given only in hospital"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment after screening must be more effective than treatment given later without screening, once the patient becomes symptomatic. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"At which levels are practice guidelines produced?","choices":["Hospital or clinic, national, and professional bodies","Only by national government agencies","Only by the journals that publish original research","Only by the individual treating clinician"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention among the national sources and bodies such as the American Academy of Pediatrics among the professional organizations. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which pair of methods is applied only in the design phase?","choices":["Simple and multivariable adjustment","Randomization and restriction","Stratification and randomization","Matching and stratification"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: both are built into the study plan, whereas matching works at either phase and the remaining methods are analysis-phase methods. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is a cohort?","choices":["A population measured at a single point in time","A group defined by whether it has the disease","A group randomly assigned to an intervention","A group with a common characteristic, followed over time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the shared characteristic can be almost anything: age, date of birth, exposure to a risk factor, occupation, religious affiliation, a disease, or taking part in an intervention. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which is an example of a cross-sectional survey?","choices":["Comparing people with lung cancer to people without for past smoking","Varicella titres showing what proportion of a population is immune","Assigning patients to a vaccine or a placebo","Following smokers for twenty years to observe lung cancer"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside determining obesity in a population at a point in time and colorectal cancer screening by fecal occult blood testing. — 2 PAJ5512 Study Design and Research Methods MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is done with the two groups once subjects have been randomly assigned?","choices":["The research question is chosen","They are followed forward and the outcome measured","Risks are then compared using relative risk","Subjects are drawn from the wider population"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the risk of the outcome is then calculated in each treatment group and compared between the groups. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How should a clinician judge whether they have good information?","choices":["By critically analyzing it and distinguishing strong from weak","By accepting anything published in a medical journal","By checking whether it agrees with what they already believe","By counting how many colleagues share the same view"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and two other things bear on it: a trusting patient-provider relationship, and the quality of the source material behind the provider's own knowledge. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What does a large number needed to treat mean?","choices":["The intervention is very safe","Many must be treated for one to benefit","Most treated patients benefit","The intervention causes frequent harm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it suggests a small absolute benefit per patient treated, whereas a small number needed to treat means the intervention is more effective. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What can give rise to confounding?","choices":["Attrition alone","Selection bias or chance","Failure to blind the outcome assessor","Publication bias alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and this is why it is an important consideration during data analysis: whether it arose by biased sampling or by bad luck, the analysis is where it has to be detected and addressed. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What does the first screening of a population detect?","choices":["Only fast-growing disease","Prevalent cases","Only false-positive cases","Incident cases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The first screening finds the disease already present in the population, and prevalence counts everyone affected at one point in time. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is the first step of evidence-based practice?","choices":["Evaluating performance or outcome","Evaluating the validity of the research","Creating a focused clinical question","Systematically reviewing the literature"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and everything after it depends on that focus: the review, the validity assessment, the application to the patient and the evaluation of outcome. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does clinical epidemiology do?","choices":["Makes predictions about individual patients from events in groups of similar patients","Replaces the history and physical examination with statistical reasoning","Describes disease frequency in a population without reference to individuals","Studies disease mechanisms in laboratory animal models"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the qualifier matters: strong scientific methods are what make those predictions accurate, so the method by which the group was studied determines how much the prediction is worth. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does the 1976 Philadelphia Legionnaires' disease outbreak illustrate?","choices":["An endemic disease confined by geography","A prevalence survey of a community","A slowly developing pandemic","An epidemic curve tied to a single gathering"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: cases were plotted by date of onset across July and August, following an American Legion convention. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which is an example of cluster sampling?","choices":["Every tenth patient on a clinic list","All patients in designated hospital units","Equal numbers of men and women","Names drawn at random from a registry"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: clusters are selected on membership in a group, such as all seniors in support groups or all students in designated classrooms. — 8 PAJ5512 Research and Trials MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is a type I error?","choices":["Stating there is a difference when there is not","Stating there is no difference when there is","Measuring the outcome incorrectly","Finding a difference that truly exists"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: a false positive, also called an alpha error, which is the error most medical research is concerned with and is judged through the p value. — 9 PAJ5512 Statistics Causation MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does attributable risk measure?","choices":["Incidence in the whole population","How many times likelier exposed persons are","Excess incidence in the exposed group","Odds of exposure among cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: it is the incidence in exposed persons minus the incidence in non-exposed persons, the possibility of disease following exposure. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which of these appears among the named sources of medical information?","choices":["Vital statistics registration","State disease registries","Pharmaceutical representatives","The United Nations Demographic Yearbook"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and their presence on the list is not an endorsement: the list is deliberately broad, and quality has to be judged separately by peer review and appraisal. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Of 240 people who have a disease, 18 die of it. What is the case-fatality ratio?","choices":["13.3 percent","0.75 percent","92.5 percent","7.5 percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: 18 deaths divided by 240 people with the disease is 0.075. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"On the timeline of disease, where does secondary prevention act?","choices":["Only at the moment of onset","Before any disease exists","Throughout the clinical course","While disease is asymptomatic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The timeline runs from no disease, to asymptomatic disease, to the clinical course, and early detection belongs to the middle phase. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"How is the negative likelihood ratio calculated?","choices":["Specificity divided by sensitivity","True negatives divided by all negatives","Sensitivity divided by one minus specificity","One minus sensitivity, divided by specificity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, being the probability of a negative result in disease over the probability of a negative result without it. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which is a way to minimize measurement bias?","choices":["Restricting the sample to one clinic","Stratifying the analysis by sex","Blinding those who assess the outcome","Matching the groups on age"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, together with strict criteria for whether the outcome occurred and equal effort to discover outcomes in all groups. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What does a sensitivity of 0.5 mean?","choices":["Half the healthy patients test negative","The test is half as expensive as the gold standard","Half the patients testing positive have the disease","An equal chance of identifying a diseased patient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A value of one identifies every patient with the disease, and a value of zero identifies none of them. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What must be true about screening benefits and harms?","choices":["Harms are acceptable if benefits are possible","Costs may be ignored","Benefits must outweigh costs and harms","Benefit need only be shown in the laboratory"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Screening exposes many healthy people to risk, so the balance has to favor benefit. — 8 PAJ5512 Prevention MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Why may the validity of a narrative review be questionable?","choices":["It states its search method in detail","It lists every excluded study and the reason","It is unstructured","It weights each study by its size"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because with no set method for finding, choosing or appraising studies, a reader cannot judge how reliable the conclusions are. — 10 PAJ5512 Clinical Questions and Reviews MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What stages are named in the progression toward death?","choices":["Exposure, incubation, infection, recovery","Nominal, ordinal, interval, continuous","Endemic, epidemic, pandemic, eradication","Susceptibility, pre-symptomatic, clinical disease, dysfunction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and setting them out as stages is what makes the point: there is no single moment at which a person crosses from well to ill. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which tool is used to estimate the pre-test probability of streptococcal pharyngitis?","choices":["The likelihood ratio nomogram","The receiver operating characteristic curve","The modified Centor score","The 2x2 table"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, scoring temperature above 38 degrees, absence of cough, tender anterior cervical nodes, tonsillar swelling or exudate, and age. — 4 PAJ5512 Tests MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"In a cohort, 12 of 300 exposed and 16 of 400 unexposed persons developed a disease. What does this show?","choices":["Exposure lowers the risk by a quarter","Exposure raises the risk by a third","No association with the exposure","The counts are insufficient to judge"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: 12 of 300 and 16 of 400 are both 0.04, so the relative risk is 1.0, the null value, which indicates no association between exposure and disease. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How is net benefit defined?","choices":["Benefit minus harm in a general primary care population","The difference in cost between offering and withholding the service","The proportion of patients who improve after the service","The statistical significance of the effect in the pooled analysis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the population matters: the service is assessed as implemented in general primary care rather than in the selected setting of a trial. — 2 PAJ5512 Evidence MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which age range defines the neonatal mortality rate?","choices":["Under one year","28 to 364 days","Under five years","Under 28 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with live births as the denominator. Postneonatal mortality covers 28 to 364 days, and infant mortality covers the whole first year. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Why do case-control studies need smaller samples than cohort studies?","choices":["Bias from recall of exposure is removed","Exposure is randomly assigned to subjects","Controls are not required for the comparison","Cases are picked for having the condition"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a cohort must be large because only a limited number of those followed get the condition, whereas a case-control study starts with people who already have it. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does prevalence describe?","choices":["The number of deaths per unit of population","The portion who develop a condition over a period","The proportion of those diagnosed who die","The portion of a group with a condition at one time"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the useful image is a snapshot: it is measured by counting who does and does not have the condition, which is what surveys are good at. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which pair of endpoints can be used to describe prognosis?","choices":["Cure or death","Sensitivity or specificity","Incidence or prevalence","Screening or diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and these sit alongside the rates used to describe outcomes, such as five-year survival and remission. — 7 PAJ5512 Prognosis MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which type of cohort study identifies the cohort from records and follows it forward to the present?","choices":["Case-control study","Randomized controlled trial","Historical (retrospective) cohort","Concurrent (prospective) cohort"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the cohort is assembled in the past from records and followed forward to the present, so the outcomes have already occurred. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"In the five As framework, what does Ask mean?","choices":["Systematically retrieve the available literature","Define the clinical question","Assess validity and applicability","Evaluate outcomes on patient care"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the example given is whether one treatment is better than another. Everything after it depends on the question being defined first. — 1 PAJ5512 Introduction Bias Validity MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"}]};
